10. Data-Flow Architecture
LIVE DATA FLOW
MT5 market/broker observations → MQL5 canonical snapshot.
MQL5 causal event/lifecycle updates → MQL5 feature engine.
MQL5 features + contextual external data → MQL5 regime engine.
MQL5 regime → continuation/mean-reversion hypothesis evaluation.
External Python data (CFTC/news/options/other explicitly admitted sources) → timestamped canonical context → MQL5 validation/consumption.
Candidate intent → MQL5 hard-risk gate → broker execution.
Execution/position state → MQL5 audit log, correlated by decision_id.
INTERNAL MQL5 FLOW
Raw market observations → data-quality state → causal event update → feature update → regime state → strategy hypothesis → decision gate → hard risk veto → execution → position management → audit/reconciliation.
Python never sits downstream of the final risk veto. External information enters as context; it cannot become an executable command.
11. Backtest Architecture
The documented backtest sequence is chronological: load historical data; advance a deterministic clock; construct the information set available at that exact time; update event lifecycles without future bars; build canonical features; classify regime; score the two hypotheses; apply risk/execution assumptions; simulate fill/position management; record every decision including NO_TRADE; calculate performance/robustness metrics.
MT5 Strategy Tester synchronization is specified as an MT5-driven snapshot/response loop: tester receives simulated event → MQL5 builds broker/account snapshot → publishes snapshot → external layer builds response → MQL5 receives response → MQL5 applies final risk/execution rules → simulation advances.
