18. Final Engineering Checklist
Authoritative revised CFTC agenda is the baseline; superseded agenda is not used to override it.
MQL5 owns the trading brain, final strategy decision, hard risk and execution.
Python is restricted to external information/data MQL5 cannot obtain or reliably process.
Every canonical feature has a frozen definition, unit, source and timestamp.
Every event is lifecycle-based and point-in-time; no final-state projection into earlier history.
Closed and forming bar states are explicitly separated.
VWAP/value definitions are frozen before implementation claims.
Proxy delta is explicitly labelled as proxy data.
Depth is used only when genuinely supplied; no artificial Level-2 is presented as real.
CFTC fields, release timing and futures-to-instrument mapping are frozen and tested.
CFTC cannot affect historical decisions before actual publication time.
Canonical bridge schema is versioned and incompatible changes create a new version.
Local file/localhost bridge is validated before ZeroMQ.
Python unavailable/stale/malformed responses fail safely according to the documented policy.
Risk limits are enforced independently of confidence/model output.
Position sizing uses actual stop distance and broker properties.
Broker order rejection/connection interruption cannot create duplicate orders.
Every trade/decision is correlated by decision_id and fully logged.
Deterministic replay and look-ahead tests pass before trusting research results.
Baseline, walk-forward, sensitivity, ablation and execution-stress tests are separated.
V38 implementation is not treated as production-ready until stored P0 audit defects are fixed and revalidated.
Recommendations remain clearly separated from approved requirements.
No-Invention Audit
Audit result: PASS FOR SPECIFICATION DISCIPLINE, with explicit open items preserved. No new strategy rule, data source, protocol, parameter, formula, broker capability, threshold, model interface or hardware target has been promoted to an authoritative requirement without source support. Where the documents are incomplete, this blueprint marks the item NOT SPECIFIED, REQUIRES DECISION, ENGINEERING GAP, or CONFLICTING REQUIREMENTS.
Known conflict resolved explicitly: the revised agenda contains language describing Python as a research/decision engine and a tester loop in which Python builds the decision, while the later governing project decision requires MQL5 to be the trading brain and Python to provide external information only. This blueprint does not silently erase the historical wording; it records the conflict and applies the later explicit decision as the controlling architecture.
Known implementation evidence: stored V38 forensic audits report critical data/lifecycle defects including first-observation-frozen bars, future-data leakage in sweep-reaction data, future-state/sweep-credit contamination, ever-flags/final-state contamination, and other production-readiness gaps. These findings are treated as implementation-status evidence and engineering risks, not as permission to redesign the project.
